can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\nC:\\Users\\jerom\\Desktop\\Food-Wrapped\\src\\components\\FeedTabs\\FeedTabs.tsx:504:7\n  502 |     if (!focusUserId && !isCustomList) return;\n  503 |     if (hasEntryFilter && !entryIdsFilter?.length) {\n> 504 |       setMonthOptions([{ value: 'all', label: 'Todo' }]);\n      |       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  505 |       return;\n  506 |     }\n  507 |     let cancelled = false;","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\nC:\\Users\\jerom\\Desktop\\Food-Wrapped\\src\\components\\FeedTabs\\FeedTabs.tsx:766:5\n  764 |     if (headerOnly) return;\n  765 |     const entryIds = entries.map((entry) => entry.id);\n> 766 |     loadEntryReactions(entryIds);\n      |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  767 |   }, [entries, headerOnly, loadEntryReactions]);\n  768 |\n  769 |   const handleToggleLike = async (entryId: string) => {","@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any","Unexpected any. Specify a different type.","TSAnyKeyword","unexpectedAny",["186","187"],"Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\nC:\\Users\\jerom\\Desktop\\Food-Wrapped\\src\\components\\NotificationsDrawer\\NotificationsDrawer.tsx:214:5\n  212 |   useEffect(() => {\n  213 |     if (!open) return;\n> 214 |     loadNotifications();\n      |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  215 |   }, [loadNotifications, open]);\n  216 |\n  217 |   useEffect(() => {","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\nC:\\Users\\jerom\\Desktop\\Food-Wrapped\\src\\components\\SavedPostsPage\\SavedPostsPage.tsx:45:5\n  43 |\n  44 |   useEffect(() => {\n> 45 |     loadSavedEntries();\n     |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  46 |   }, [loadSavedEntries]);\n  47 |\n  48 |   useEffect(() => {","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\nC:\\Users\\jerom\\Desktop\\Food-Wrapped\\src\\components\\UserDashboardPage\\UserDashboardPage.tsx:74:5\n  72 |\n  73 |   useEffect(() => {\n> 74 |     loadProfile();\n     |     ^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  75 |   }, [loadProfile]);\n  76 |\n  77 |   useEffect(() => {","Error: Calling setState synchronously within an effect can trigger cascading renders\n\nEffects are intended to synchronize state between React and external systems such as manually updating the DOM, state management libraries, or other platform APIs. In general, the body of an effect should do one or both of the following:\n* Update external systems with the latest state from React.\n* Subscribe for updates from some external system, calling setState in a callback function when external state changes.\n\nCalling setState synchronously within an effect body causes cascading renders that can hurt performance, and is not recommended. (https://react.dev/learn/you-might-not-need-an-effect).\n\nC:\\Users\\jerom\\Desktop\\Food-Wrapped\\src\\components\\UserDashboardPage\\UserDashboardPage.tsx:163:5\n  161 |\n  162 |   useEffect(() => {\n> 163 |     loadEntries();\n      |     ^^^^^^^^^^^ Avoid calling setState() directly within an effect\n  164 |   }, [loadEntries]);\n  165 |\n  166 |   useRevalidateOnFocus(","react-hooks/exhaustive-deps","React Hook useEffect has a spread element in its dependency array. This means we can't statically verify whether you've passed the correct dependencies.","SpreadElement",{"messageId":"188","fix":"189","desc":"190"},{"messageId":"191","fix":"192","desc":"193"},"suggestUnknown",{"range":"194","text":"195"},"Use `unknown` instead, this will force you to explicitly, and safely assert the type is correct.","suggestNever",{"range":"196","text":"197"},"Use `never` instead, this is useful when instantiating generic type parameters that you don't need to know the type of.",[2484,2487],"unknown",[2484,2487],"never"]